---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// Figures are measured from the repo (public/data/skyline.json, scripts/measure-hero-size.mjs), not estimated.
const facts = [
  { n: '6,832', label: 'buildings in the scene' },
  { n: '11,442', label: 'trees, 1,893 boats' },
  { n: '~490 KB', label: 'hero code + data, gzipped' },
  { n: '$0', label: 'hosting, data and tools' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How I built the skyline"
  description="A case study: how the interactive Vancouver skyline on this site was built from open data, hand-modelled landmarks and AI-assisted development, and kept fast enough for a phone."
>
  <p class="label"><a href="/">Home</a> / Case study</p>
  <h1>How I built the skyline</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The skyline on the homepage isn't a video or a stock photo. It's a real-time 3D model of downtown Vancouver, built from
    city open data and running in your browser. This is how it came together, and what I'd do differently.
  </p>

  <dl class="facts">
    {facts.map((f) => <div><dt>{f.label}</dt><dd>{f.n}</dd></div>)}
  </dl>

  <h2>The brief</h2>
  <p>
    I wanted the first screen of my site to say what I do: translate between people with a goal and the people who build the
    thing. A skyline is a good metaphor for that, and Vancouver is home. The constraints were strict. It had to cost nothing to
    host, run entirely in the browser, stay smooth on a mid-range phone, and still work for anyone who can't or won't run 3D.
  </p>
  <p>
    I'm a journalist and communicator, not a graphics programmer. I wrote the brief, made the creative and technical decisions,
    and directed the build with Claude Code. That's what I mean by AI-assisted development: I'm the editor and product owner,
    and the AI does the typing.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Start with real data</h2>
  <p>
    The buildings come from the City of Vancouver's open data portal. Its 2015 footprints give accurate outlines but no heights.
    The 2009 dataset has heights, including the stepped shapes of podiums and towers. OpenStreetMap fills in towers built after
    both datasets were published. A script in the repo stitches the three together:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>If 2009 parts cover most of a 2015 footprint, it's the same building, so use the 2009 stepped shape.</li>
    <li>Otherwise use the 2015 outline with a height from OpenStreetMap, or an estimate if nobody has one.</li>
    <li>If OpenStreetMap says the site is now much taller, treat it as a redevelopment and use that geometry.</li>
    <li>Any OpenStreetMap building that matches no 2015 footprint is new, so add it.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The script runs on my machine, not in your browser. It crops to the view from the Olympic Village seawall, simplifies every
    outline, and writes one compact data file. The page never calls the city's servers.
  </p>
  <p>
    The North Shore mountains use real terrain from Canada's national elevation model, and the shoreline, bridge, SkyTrain line,
    marinas and street grid all come from the same pipeline.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Hand-build what data can't describe</h2>
  <p>
    Footprints don't know about domes, saucers or glowing rings. BC Place, Science World and Harbour Centre are modelled by hand
    as simple shapes and placed with their real coordinates, so they read at a glance. Those same landmarks double as the site's
    navigation: Harbour Centre leads to About, the tallest downtown tower to Journalism, and so on.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Make it look like a photograph</h2>
  <p>
    I decided early that this should look real, not stylized. It's blue hour, about twenty minutes after sunset. The theme
    colours, orange and black, come from the lighting and the interface rather than from painting buildings orange.
    Windows are generated floor by floor, with some lit and some dark. Red lights blink on the tallest towers. The water
    reflects the city, and there's bloom, fog and a little film grain to finish.
  </p>
  <p>
    Several choices came from looking at the picture and arguing with it. The camera started 40 metres up. At 100 metres the
    downtown core and signature towers clear Yaletown, so that's where it sits now.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Keep it fast</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A poster first.</strong> A pre-rendered image of the same view loads instantly. The 3D scene fades in over it.</li>
    <li><strong>Load 3D late.</strong> It starts on the first scroll, touch or key press, or a few seconds after the page loads, so it stays out of the critical loading window.</li>
    <li><strong>Adaptive quality.</strong> The scene measures its own frame rate and steps down detail if it's struggling. Phones idle at 30 frames per second and cap their pixel density.</li>
    <li><strong>Draw less.</strong> Buildings out of view aren't drawn, and distant ones use simpler detail.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The result: about 490 KB gzipped for all hero code and data, against a 700 KB budget, and a mobile Lighthouse performance
    score of 99 on the homepage when I last measured it.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Always have a way out</h2>
  <p>
    If someone prefers reduced motion, has no WebGL or has JavaScript off, they get the still poster and the normal navigation.
    There's a visible "Skip to work" link, the landmarks are reachable by keyboard, and there's a plain menu. A cool effect
    shouldn't be the only way in.
  </p>

  <h2>What's still rough</h2>
  <p>
    Heights are only as good as the datasets. The City publishes 2022 LiDAR that would give accurate current heights, but its
    download server blocks scripts, so those tiles have to be fetched by hand in a browser. That's the next upgrade. Some
    newer towers are also approximated from OpenStreetMap.
  </p>

  <h2>What I took from it</h2>
  <p>
    The biggest lesson was about translation. Most of the work wasn't code. It was deciding what "realistic" means, choosing
    which data to trust, setting a performance budget and checking the result against it. Those are the same skills I use
    between business teams and developers every day.
  </p>

  <p class="credit">
    Data: City of Vancouver Open Data (Open Government Licence – Vancouver), © OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL), and Natural
    Resources Canada's elevation model. Full attribution is in the footer. The code is on
    <a href="https://github.com/realjacobvanluven/jacobvanluven-site">GitHub</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lede {
    max-width: 44rem;
    font-size: 1.1875rem;
  }

  .facts {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 11rem), 1fr));
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin: 2rem 0;
  }

  .facts div {
    padding: 1rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: 8px;
  }

  .facts dt {
    order: 2;
    font: 500 0.75rem var(--font-mono);
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .facts dd {
    margin: 0 0 0.25rem;
    font: 700 1.75rem var(--font-display);
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .facts div {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  h2 {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }

  .lede,
  p,
  ol,
  ul {
    max-width: 44rem;
  }

  li {
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }

  .credit {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
